WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:04.559
Welcome to Module Three: GPU Acceleration and Optimization.

2
00:00:04.889 --> 00:00:08.551
Here you learn how language models are really trained on GPUs.

3
00:00:09.351 --> 00:00:12.672
Picture a startup that has just rented eight H100s.

4
00:00:13.002 --> 00:00:17.283
The plan: fully fine-tune a thirteen billion parameter model by Friday.

5
00:00:18.083 --> 00:00:21.467
The first launch dies in seconds with an out-of-memory error.

6
00:00:21.797 --> 00:00:25.481
Someone halves the batch size, and it crashes exactly the same way.

7
00:00:26.281 --> 00:00:30.177
In bf16, the weights alone are only twenty-six gigabytes.

8
00:00:30.507 --> 00:00:35.556
But AdamW also keeps gradients, a master copy, and two optimizer moments.

9
00:00:36.356 --> 00:00:40.082
That's sixteen bytes per parameter, about two hundred and eight gigabytes.

10
00:00:40.412 --> 00:00:44.757
No single eighty gigabyte GPU holds that, whatever the batch size.

11
00:00:45.557 --> 00:00:50.307
Lesson one teaches this memory math, from optimizer states to the KV cache.

12
00:00:50.637 --> 00:00:54.235
You'll write an estimator that answers "will it fit?" before you launch.

13
00:00:55.035 --> 00:00:56.943
Lesson two adds mixed precision,

14
00:00:56.993 --> 00:01:00.126
gradient accumulation, and activation checkpointing.

15
00:01:00.456 --> 00:01:04.246
FlashAttention and torch.compile then make every step faster.

16
00:01:05.046 --> 00:01:09.561
In lesson three's lab, you'll pretrain a small Llama from scratch on TinyStories.

17
00:01:09.891 --> 00:01:13.617
You'll watch the loss fall, then generate stories from your own checkpoint.

18
00:01:14.417 --> 00:01:16.842
Back at the startup, the real fix is sharding.

19
00:01:17.172 --> 00:01:22.519
With FSDP or ZeRO, each GPU keeps only its own slice of the training state.

20
00:01:23.319 --> 00:01:28.347
Lesson four starts with simple data parallelism, using DDP and torchrun.

21
00:01:28.677 --> 00:01:33.640
Then come tensor and pipeline parallelism, for models too big for one GPU.

22
00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:39.574
Placement matters: tensor parallelism stays inside a node, on fast NVLink.

23
00:01:39.904 --> 00:01:45.507
Pipeline stages and data-parallel replicas can stretch across nodes over InfiniBand.

24
00:01:46.307 --> 00:01:51.548
The job finally runs, and the dashboard shows 100% GPU utilization.

25
00:01:51.878 --> 00:01:55.561
Yet progress is slow, because the Tensor Cores are mostly waiting.

26
00:01:56.361 --> 00:02:01.410
Lesson five measures real efficiency with model FLOPs utilization, or MFU.

27
00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:05.231
You'll profile steps with PyTorch and Nsight to find the stalls.

28
00:02:06.031 --> 00:02:09.480
You'll learn an ordered ladder of fixes for out-of-memory errors.

29
00:02:09.810 --> 00:02:13.045
And you'll debug NaN losses, loss spikes, and NCCL hangs.

30
00:02:13.845 --> 00:02:16.142
This domain is fourteen percent of the exam.

31
00:02:16.472 --> 00:02:21.521
Expect memory math, ZeRO stages, parallel layouts, and profiling scenarios.

32
00:02:22.321 --> 00:02:26.537
By the end, you'll size, launch, scale, and debug a real training run.

33
00:02:26.867 --> 00:02:29.911
Let's start with lesson one: GPU memory math.
